<meta type=article title="Frameable build fixture (Phase 3 slice 3b)">
</meta>

<section | The frameable class>

Phase 3 slice 3b builds the frameable shared capability. Three new vocab
entries (`<fig>`, `<svg>`, `<frame>`) join the existing handlers
(`<table>`, `<csv>`, `<tsv>`, `<mermaid>`, `<abc>`) in the frameable
class. They share a frameable surface (id, title, caption, border,
numbered) and the same `formatLabel` primitive for their number-label
rendering.

<sub-section | The `<fig>` canonical form>

`<fig>` is the canonical Layer 1 name; `<figure>` is an authoring alias
that rewrites to `<fig>` at the normalize-to-canonical gate.

<fig #fig:elephant src=elephant.jpg | An African elephant.>

The alias form:

<figure src=zebra.jpg | A zebra in the savanna.>

Both render as HTML-native `<figure>` elements. The handler decides the
output tagname; the vocab key (and the AST tagname after the gate
rewrite) is `fig` regardless.

<sub-section | DSL counter assignments>

Slice 3a wired the math envs into the equation counter. Slice 3b
extends NUMBERED_TAGNAMES with the DSL handlers' counter assignments:
`<csv>` and `<tsv>` share the `table` counter with `<table>`; `<mermaid>`
and `<abc>` share the `figure` counter with `<fig>`.

A standalone CSV with a table cross-reference:

<csv #tab:salaries caption="Software engineer salary by level">
level,median,p90
junior,90000,120000
senior,160000,210000
staff,260000,340000
</csv>

A mermaid diagram on the figure counter:

<mermaid #fig:flow caption="Compilation flow">
graph LR
  A[Source] --> B[Parser]
  B --> C[AST]
  C --> D[HTML]
</mermaid>

Cross-references resolve with the right prefixes — <ref @tab:salaries>
resolves to "table 3" (1: the `<fig src=elephant.jpg>` aliased above
counts as a `<fig>` registration; 2: the zebra; ... actually wait,
the table counter is independent — this is "table 1"). And
<ref @fig:flow> resolves to "figure 3" (1: elephant, 2: zebra, 3: flow).

<sub-section | Theorem-family label rendering>

Slice 3b's theorem-family handler renders the "Theorem N (Name)." label
spans that slice 3a left deferred (the registry-side numbering landed in
3a; the visible labels in 3b).

<theorem #thm:fundamental name="Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic">
Every integer greater than 1 either is prime itself or is the product
of prime numbers, and that this product is unique up to ordering.
</theorem>

<lemma>
Every nonempty subset of the natural numbers has a least element.
</lemma>

<proof | By induction on the size of the subset.>

Cross-reference to <ref @thm:fundamental> resolves to "theorem 1".

<sub-section | The +border frameable surface>

The `+border` flag opts a `<fig>` into the frameable-border class. Off
by default for `<fig>`.

<fig +border src=plot.png | A plot with a frame around it.>

The class flows through to the rendered `<figure>` so theme stylesheets
can target it.
